Haiku kernel: an mmap() can panic the machine through _RandomizeAddress().

    PANIC: ASSERT FAILED (src/system/kernel/vm/VMUserAddressSpace.cpp:465):
           start <= end
    Thread 24195 "NetworkService" running on CPU 0

      VMUserAddressSpace::_RandomizeAddress(...)
      VMUserAddressSpace::_InsertAreaSlot(0x100000, 524288, ...)
      VMUserAddressSpace::InsertArea(...)
      vm_map_cache(...)
      vm_map_file(6, ..., 524288, ...)
      _user_map_file
      handle_syscall
      <content_shell>

Seen on hrev99002+148 (x86_gcc2, 2 GB machine) while Chromium 114 loaded
x.com. The mapping is one of Chromium's shared memory regions -- 524288 bytes,
mmap of a descriptor -- and the address specification is
B_RANDOMIZED_ANY_ADDRESS, so searchBase is USER_BASE_ANY (0x100000) and
searchEnd is fEndAddress.

A userland mmap() should not be able to take the kernel down, whatever state
the address space is in.

## What happens

_InsertAreaSlot() walks the areas looking for a hole. When it does not find
one it falls through to the last resort: the reserved areas. There, for a
reserved area big enough to hold the request at its end, it picks a randomised
base inside the area (VMUserAddressSpace.cpp, around line 765):

    addr_t startRange = next->Base() + next->Size();
    startRange -= size + kMaxRandomize;
    startRange = ROUNDDOWN(startRange, alignment);
    startRange = std::max(startRange, alignedNextBase);

    addr_t rangeEnd
        = std::min(next->Base() + next->Size() - size, end);
    alignedBase = _RandomizeAddress(startRange, rangeEnd, alignment);

The second line is an unguarded subtraction on unsigned addresses. On 32-bit,
kMaxRandomize is 0x800000 -- 8 MB -- so whenever

    next->Base() + next->Size() < size + 8 MB

it underflows and startRange becomes an address near 2^32. ROUNDDOWN keeps it
there, std::max() keeps it there, and _RandomizeAddress() is then called with
a start above its end.

That condition is not exotic. USER_BASE_ANY is 0x100000, so any reserved area
sitting in the low 8.5 MB of the address space satisfies it for a 512 KB
request. What makes it rare is only that this branch is reached when the
ordinary search has already failed -- an address space that is full or badly
fragmented, which is what a 32-bit browser produces after a few hundred
mappings.

The other five _RandomizeAddress() call sites in this function are each
guarded by is_valid_spot(), which establishes alignedBase + size - 1 <= limit
and therefore start <= end. This one is not: it computes its own start.

## On a release kernel

ASSERT is compiled out below KDEBUG_LEVEL 2, so on a release kernel there is
no panic and no diagnostic. _RandomizeAddress() instead computes

    addr_t range = end - start + 1;

which underflows in turn, giving a range of nearly 2^32, and returns
start + (random % range) -- an arbitrary address, which is then used as the
base of the new area. That is worse than the panic, not better.

## The fix

Do the subtraction only when it cannot underflow. When it would, the clamp
that follows is the answer anyway: alignedNextBase is already known to satisfy
alignedNextBase + size - 1 <= min(next->Base() + next->Size() - 1, end), from
the is_valid_spot() test that guards this branch, so it is a valid start and
it is <= rangeEnd.

--- a/src/system/kernel/vm/VMUserAddressSpace.cpp
+++ b/src/system/kernel/vm/VMUserAddressSpace.cpp
@@ -762,8 +762,17 @@
 							addr_t alignedNextBase = align_address(next->Base(),
 								alignment);
 
 							addr_t startRange = next->Base() + next->Size();
-							startRange -= size + kMaxRandomize;
+							// Unsigned, and kMaxRandomize is 8 MB on 32-bit:
+							// a reserved area low in the address space makes
+							// this underflow, and _RandomizeAddress() is then
+							// called with a start above its end. The clamp
+							// below is the right answer in that case --
+							// alignedNextBase is a valid start, by the
+							// is_valid_spot() test that guards this branch.
+							if (startRange < size + kMaxRandomize)
+								startRange = alignedNextBase;
+							else
+								startRange -= size + kMaxRandomize;
 							startRange = ROUNDDOWN(startRange, alignment);
 							startRange = std::max(startRange, alignedNextBase);
 
